#heading(numbering: none, outlined: false)[Abstract]

A contended lock decides whose critical section, the code a thread runs while holding the lock, runs next. Patel et al. showed that granting turns in arrival order is not enough: threads with longer critical sections take more of the lock and subvert the CPU scheduler. Usage-fair locks address this directly. u-SCL, Patel et al.'s user-space lock, measures how long each thread holds the lock, gives each thread in turn a slice of a few milliseconds of exclusive ownership, and makes threads that overuse the lock sleep; CFL, a lock modeled on Linux's CFS CPU scheduler, reorders the wait queue so that the thread that has held the lock least goes next. Both restore a fair allocation in real systems. Both, however, keep the thread being served as the thread that runs the critical section, so every fair hand-off moves the protected data to another core, and both win back locality by giving up some fairness: u-SCL lets a slice owner reacquire the lock for milliseconds, and CFL prefers waiters on the same socket. Each also needs a rule for a thread that is owed service but is not waiting when the lock decides, for example because it is still waking up: u-SCL holds the lock idle for its slice owner, while CFL gives the turn to whoever is waiting. We call these two costs _migration_ and _absence_. Delegation locks remove migration: threads publish their critical sections as requests, and one thread, the combiner, runs them all where the data already is. Absence remains, and becomes an explicit waiting policy. We present FC-PQ, a delegation lock whose combiner keeps pending requests in a priority queue and serves the one whose thread has accumulated the least critical-section time. We evaluate it inside redb, an embedded key-value store that runs one write transaction at a time, with the CPU clock fixed at 3.0 GHz. When 1-record and 64-record writers compete, FC-PQ raises Jain's fairness index over critical-section time (1 means equal shares) from 0.85--0.86 under MCS, a queue lock that serves waiters in arrival order, to 0.94--0.995. It also runs 1.17--1.34× as many transactions per second and fetches 10--18 cache lines from other cores per transaction where MCS fetches 93--126. It ties u-SCL, because redb's writers resubmit at once and are never absent. Making FC-PQ's waiters sleep instead of spin exposes absence directly: with identical transactions, sleeping matches u-SCL's CPU time at spinning throughput, but with mixed sizes short-transaction clients wake too late and fairness falls as low as 0.65.
